    {
      "id": "cand-a1-6a-0044",
      "turkish": "merkezî",
      "english": "central",
      "priority": 3,
      "tags": [
        "adjective",
        "unit-a1-6a",
        "unit-a1",
        "pronunciation"
      ],
      "source": "A1_-_6A.pdf",
      "notes": "Shows three things spelling hides at once: the first e is the open [æ] of a closed syllable before r, the second the close [e] of an open syllable; the k is palatalised to [c] before a front vowel; the Arabic nisba -î is long. Convention: IPA, with ˈ before the stressed syllable, ː for length, [æ] vs [e] for the two e qualities, [c]/[ɟ] for palatalised k/g, and [l] vs [ɫ] for clear and dark l. Transcribed by hand — TDK supplies length, stress and a prose note on l, but not the vowel allophony or the k/g palatalisation, so this cannot be generated. Transcription removed. It was produced by applying my own rules — \"closed syllable gives æ\", \"ğ lengthens\" — rather than from evidence, and checking against en. and tr.wiktionary showed both rules over-generate: æ is conditioned by a following sonorant (ders, benzer) and not by a closed syllable as such, so any e before k, t, p or ş was wrong; and ğ lengthens only before a consonant, not between vowels. Left empty rather than guessed. Audio is in resources/pron_audio/ and scripts/check_pron.py looks the word up, so this can be restored from a source. Restored after the withdrawal was found to be an over-correction: the æ before a sonorant is attested (en.wiktionary gives element /eleˈmænt/, ders /dæɾs/, cömert /dʒœˈmæɾt/) and the learner confirms it by ear. Only the æ before an obstruent was wrong. Where Wiktionary writes /e/ in this position it is transcribing broadly, which is not evidence against the allophone.",
      "status": "approved",
      "pron_tr": "mæɾceˈziː"
    }
  ]
